### What is the structure of multi-level caching?

The hierarchy is L1 (Caffeine, in-process, nanoseconds) in front of L2 (Redis, shared, sub-millisecond) in front of the database (source of truth, milliseconds). A read flows L1 then L2 then DB, populating upward on return.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class MultiLevelCacheDemo {

    private final Map<String, BigDecimal> l1 = new HashMap<>();  // Caffeine per-instance
    private final Map<String, BigDecimal> l2 = new HashMap<>();  // Redis shared
    private final Map<String, BigDecimal> db = Map.of(
            "SAV2001", new BigDecimal("75000.00"));

    public BigDecimal get(String accountId) {
        // Check L1 first (nanoseconds)
        BigDecimal val = l1.get(accountId);
        if (val != null) { System.out.println("L1 HIT (nanoseconds)"); return val; }

        // Check L2 (sub-millisecond network hop)
        val = l2.get(accountId);
        if (val != null) {
            System.out.println("L2 HIT (Redis), populating L1");
            l1.put(accountId, val);
            return val;
        }

        // Load from DB (milliseconds)
        System.out.println("DB LOAD, populating L2 and L1");
        val = db.get(accountId);
        l2.put(accountId, val);
        l1.put(accountId, val);
        return val;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MultiLevelCacheDemo cache = new MultiLevelCacheDemo();
        cache.get("SAV2001"); // DB LOAD
        cache.get("SAV2001"); // L1 HIT
    }
}
```

```text
DB LOAD, populating L2 and L1
L1 HIT (nanoseconds)
```

### How does L1 absorb a stampede when L2 expires?

With only Redis, when a hot key expires, the next read from every instance misses and hits the database simultaneously. With an L1 per instance, each instance may still have the value, so reads continue from L1 without touching Redis or the database.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

public class StampedeAbsorption {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int instances = 20;
        int concurrentRequestsPerInstance = 500;

        System.out.println("=== Without L1 (Redis only) ===");
        int dbHitsNoL1 = instances * concurrentRequestsPerInstance;
        System.out.println("Redis key expires → " + dbHitsNoL1 + " concurrent DB reloads");

        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("=== With L1 (Caffeine + single-flight) ===");
        int dbHitsWithL1 = instances; // one per instance, single-flight within each
        System.out.println("Redis key expires → each instance serves from L1");
        System.out.println("Only " + dbHitsWithL1 + " DB reloads (one per instance)");
        System.out.println("Reduction: " + dbHitsNoL1 + " → " + dbHitsWithL1);
    }
}
```

```text
=== Without L1 (Redis only) ===
Redis key expires → 10000 concurrent DB reloads

=== With L1 (Caffeine + single-flight) ===
Redis key expires → each instance serves from L1
Only 20 DB reloads (one per instance)
Reduction: 10000 → 20
```

### What is the L1 TTL consistency trade-off?

Because L1 is per-instance and not invalidated when another instance writes, a value in L1 can be stale for up to its TTL even after Redis and the database were updated.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

public class L1TtlTradeoff {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Short L1 TTL (2 seconds):");
        System.out.println("  + Instances re-check Redis every 2s → fresh data");
        System.out.println("  - Instances fall through to Redis often → weaker stampede protection");

        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Long L1 TTL (30 seconds):");
        System.out.println("  + Instances serve from L1 for 30s → strong DB protection");
        System.out.println("  - A change can take up to 30s to become visible → more staleness");

        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Choose based on how much staleness the data tolerates:");
        System.out.println("  Displayed balance aggregate → 2-5s L1 TTL is fine");
        System.out.println("  Near-real-time rate → very short L1 TTL or no L1");
    }
}
```

```text
Short L1 TTL (2 seconds):
  + Instances re-check Redis every 2s → fresh data
  - Instances fall through to Redis often → weaker stampede protection

Long L1 TTL (30 seconds):
  + Instances serve from L1 for 30s → strong DB protection
  - A change can take up to 30s to become visible → more staleness

Choose based on how much staleness the data tolerates:
  Displayed balance aggregate → 2-5s L1 TTL is fine
  Near-real-time rate → very short L1 TTL or no L1
```

### How does broadcast invalidation work?

For data requiring prompt cross-instance freshness, publish an eviction message over Redis Pub/Sub so each instance evicts that key from its L1.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.util.Map;
import java.util.HashMap;

public class BroadcastInvalidation {

    private final Map<String, String> l1 = new HashMap<>();

    public void onInvalidationMessage(String key) {
        l1.remove(key);
        System.out.println("L1 evicted: " + key + " (broadcast received)");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BroadcastInvalidation instance1 = new BroadcastInvalidation();
        BroadcastInvalidation instance2 = new BroadcastInvalidation();

        instance1.l1.put("SAV2001", "75000");
        instance2.l1.put("SAV2001", "75000");

        // Writer updates Redis and publishes invalidation
        System.out.println("Writer: balance updated in Redis, publishing invalidation");
        instance1.onInvalidationMessage("SAV2001");
        instance2.onInvalidationMessage("SAV2001");
        System.out.println("Both instances now fetch fresh value on next read");
    }
}
```

```text
Writer: balance updated in Redis, publishing invalidation
L1 evicted: SAV2001 (broadcast received)
L1 evicted: SAV2001 (broadcast received)
Both instances now fetch fresh value on next read
```

### How do you compare TTL-based vs broadcast invalidation?

| Strategy | Mechanism | Staleness | Complexity | Best For |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| TTL-based | L1 entries expire after TTL | Up to L1 TTL | Simple | Data tolerating seconds of staleness |
| Broadcast (Pub/Sub) | Writer publishes eviction | Near-immediate | More machinery | Data requiring prompt freshness |
| Combined | Short TTL as backstop + broadcast | Near-immediate | Most complete | Critical shared data |

### Why must L1 be bounded?

L1 lives in the JVM heap. Without a size limit, it can consume unbounded memory and cause GC pressure or OOM.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import com.github.benmanes.caffeine.cache.Caffeine;
import com.github.benmanes.caffeine.cache.Cache;
import java.time.Duration;

public class BoundedL1 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Cache<String, String> l1 = Caffeine.newBuilder()
                .maximumSize(10_000)           // bounded by entry count
                .expireAfterWrite(Duration.ofSeconds(5)) // bounded by time
                .build();

        System.out.println("L1 bounded: max 10,000 entries, 5s TTL");
        System.out.println("Prevents unbounded heap growth per instance");
    }
}
```

```text
L1 bounded: max 10,000 entries, 5s TTL
Prevents unbounded heap growth per instance
```

### What are common mistakes?

Using an unbounded in-process cache (a plain HashMap) as L1 without size limits or TTL, causing memory leaks and eventual OOM under load.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.util.HashMap;

// WRONG: unbounded HashMap as L1
public class WrongL1 {
    static HashMap<String, String> l1 = new HashMap<>();
    // Grows forever, no eviction, no TTL → OOM under load
}

// CORRECT: Caffeine with size and time bounds
// Caffeine.newBuilder().maximumSize(10_000).expireAfterWrite(5, SECONDS).build();
```

